WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.860
- Remember that package you received

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.290
when you got into Yale?

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.450
Well it's high time you receive another.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:15.510
Since we cannot distribute
the typical items

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.180
of the Class Day celebration,
we've instead shipped

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.270
every member of our class a
special Class Day CARE Package.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.090
The box includes many of
the traditional items:

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.880
the anthology that Sarah
told us about yesterday,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.380
a Yale College pin, a handkerchief
like the one we received

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.300
during our first week at Yale,
though this time sponsored

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.130
by the Yale Alumni Association

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.860
for Bright College Years this Sunday.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.130
Some of the traditional
items we are keeping

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.290
for our future in-person ceremony

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.000
such as the clay pipes that we
traditionally smash together

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.870
on Old Campus, but don't worry.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.850
We're gonna get to do that too.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.500
The CARE Package also includes

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.470
some special elements for this year.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.170
We've added the tassels that would've come

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.010
with your Commencement gown as well as

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.160
a special Yale photo banner.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.840
Whether you're wearing a suit and tie

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.430
or you're still wearing those pajamas

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.950
you put on in March,
be sure to post a photo

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.123
with the banner and share it, #Yale2020.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.220
And now to the main attraction of the day,

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.280
the comedic reflection
by Simon Fraser, J.E.,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.030
and Oscar Lopez Aguirre, Hopper.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.760
We all could use some laughs right now

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.930
and Simon and Oscar sure can deliver.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.053
So here's to them.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.273
- Hey Oscar.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.760
Oh I think you're muted.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.100
- [Oscar] Am I, oh, sorry, thank you.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.200
Is it, am I good?

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.400
Am I good?
- You're great, you're great.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.490
Should we give this speech then?

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.490
- The one we wrote the
day before Spring Break?

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.323
- That's the one.

00:01:45.323 --> 00:01:46.410
- I don't know, do you
think it'll be an issue

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.780
that some stuff has happened since?

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.610
- I don't see it affecting
our speech that much.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.257
- Okay, let's do it.

00:01:53.255 --> 00:01:55.900
- Pray, President Salovey.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:56.733
- Dean Chun.

00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:57.790
- Michelle Obama.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:58.623
- Family.

00:01:58.623 --> 00:01:59.456
- Friends.

00:01:59.456 --> 00:02:00.690
- Uninvited cousins.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.600
- It is so great to be
in New Haven speaking

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.930
to the thousands of you so close together.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:12.530
The Elm City, the 06511,
east of West Haven,

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.070
west of East Haven, unfair of Fair Haven,

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.110
isn't it just a beautiful, beautiful day

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.460
in sunny, sunny New Haven?

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.720
There's a reason we endure
eight months of rain

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.620
and snow and gloom and improv shows

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.750
for days like these in New Haven.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.050
- Yeah Simon, sorry, I'm
actually seeing a problem

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.383
with the speech.

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:37.292
- What is it?

00:02:37.292 --> 00:02:38.150
- Well our speech is ten minutes

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.190
and currently you spend
six of those talking

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.540
about how nice it is to be in New Haven.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.373
- What's your point?

00:02:44.373 --> 00:02:46.622
- Well we're not actually in New Haven.

00:02:46.622 --> 00:02:47.550
- I am.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.180
- I thought Yale kicked us all out.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.730
- They did but old Dean of
Pearson still has a house here

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.127
and she's letting me stay in
exchange for a few favors.

00:02:54.127 --> 00:02:55.130
- What favors?

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.730
- Just some Yelp reviews.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.760
I'm just kidding, I live off campus.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:02:59.593
- Of course you do.

00:02:59.593 --> 00:03:00.426
- What?
- Nothing.

00:03:00.426 --> 00:03:01.870
You know what, should we just do

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.220
our more up-to-date speech then?

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.170
- That sounds good.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.500
We are here to celebrate
the Class of 2020.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.010
What an iconic class.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.640
Not since the class of 1010
and not until the class

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.640
of 42069 will that be a
more iconic class year.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.870
I remember when I got
accepted, early action, legacy.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.670
- Regular Decision, poor.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.610
- And I was like I'm
going to take a gap year,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.323
and then I was like, class of 2021?

00:03:33.623 --> 00:03:35.709
(mimics vomiting)

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:37.750
- Okay Simon, really, you puked?

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.050
You had a bodily response to a class year?

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.320
So immature.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.490
They are just as special
as those who came before us

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.040
like the Class of 20 (mimics vomiting).

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.800
Wait, what's happening, wait, I'm scared.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:49.633
You try it?

00:03:49.633 --> 00:03:50.649
- Class of 20 (mimics vomiting).

00:03:50.649 --> 00:03:52.160
- 20 (mimics vomiting).

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.504
Okay, I think we gotta move on then.

00:03:54.504 --> 00:03:57.200
What an almost four years we had.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.400
- This year the big
celebration was Yale 50.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.290
- That's right, Simon.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.370
Papa Johns continues to
give half off all pizzas

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:05.573
with the code "Yale50".

00:04:05.573 --> 00:04:06.650
What a deal!

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.430
- No, no Oscar, Yale celebrated 50 years

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.770
of women being at the college,

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.070
which is crazy when you think about it

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.720
because us guys are only here for four.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.340
- Coming to Yale was daunting.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:21.520
Before we arrived on campus,
we didn't know so many things.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:22.910
We didn't know if we were going

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:24.440
to be friends with our roommates,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.740
we didn't know what
classes we were gonna take,

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.120
we didn't know if we were going to fit in,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.810
but we did know that even
if everything else failed,

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.142
we would all have at least one friend.

00:04:34.142 --> 00:04:35.643
- George Iskander!

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:37.893
- Hey George, how's it going?

00:04:37.893 --> 00:04:39.824
- It's going well guys, just eating some--

00:04:39.824 --> 00:04:41.024
- That's enough, George.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:44.700
In our four years, we have seen so much

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.020
and we have achieved so much.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.949
In our class we have Rhodes
Scholars and Woads Scholars.

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:53.810
- We have finance bros and astrology hoes,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.900
I'm in the latter, varsity
athletes who put their heart

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.460
and soul into their sport.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.750
We also have the rugby team.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:03.933
- We have actors and writers,
new journalists and the YDN.

00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:07.350
- We have CCE instructors
who taught us about consent

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.260
by having us ask another person

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.540
if they wanted to get Froyo.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.230
- Since then, only my FOOT
group has asked me to get Froyo?

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.160
- You didn't do FOOT?

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.060
- That's the joke.

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:18.980
- And we have friends in acapella groups

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.767
who taught about consent when they say,

00:05:20.767 --> 00:05:22.716
"You really don't have to
come if you don't want to,"

00:05:22.716 --> 00:05:23.730
and we didn't.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.143
- We've had grade inflation.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.812
- Though not as much as that
smuck ol' school in Cambridge.

00:05:28.812 --> 00:05:31.645
(forced laughing)

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.680
- We had Feb club.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.060
What a money-making sham that was.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.000
I paid $29 to only go to the first party,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.341
which was at my house.

00:05:43.341 --> 00:05:45.020
- Speaking of paying for parties,

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.420
are we getting that $125
in your dues refund?

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.900
Last time I checked,
making TikTok was free.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.910
- In our years at Yale, we've
had world renowned scholars

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.466
teach us critical thinking
in all different fields.

00:05:57.466 --> 00:06:00.110
- And now most of us will
be applying to those fields

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.340
to make the world of
finance a better place.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:05.370
I however am actually
using my critical thinking

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.100
to question why the Lily
Pad is on top of Toad's.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.830
If the toad's drowning,
who's gonna save it.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.310
Yes I'm unemployed, but aren't we all?

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:18.944
- Oscar, it's not the Lily
Pad, it's actually Lily's Pad?

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:20.840
- Well, who's Lily?

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.380
Is her name short of Lillian or Liliana?

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:24.590
Is that the toad's name?

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.750
It must be, I took first order logic.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.930
- While we were here, we won the Game

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.283
three out of four times.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.030
- And the one time we lost
was on a baseball field.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:38.340
Now I may have just learned
that a "down" isn't when

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:39.960
a football player is feeling sad,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.330
but I'm pretty sure that
means it doesn't count.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.690
Every touchdown was a home run.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:46.420
During this most recent game,

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.520
a group of Yale students united
to protest climate change.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.316
- And Puerto Rican debt, Oscar.

00:06:51.316 --> 00:06:53.930
- Okay, but I'm Latino
so I can't say that.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.040
This protest caused some criticism

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.950
and the criticism went like this.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.110
Why would these students decide to protest

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.800
the most hollowed of sporting events

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.350
that would maximize
viewership and exposure

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:06.800
not only on campus but the alumni

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.750
and those watching the
game across the nation,

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.723
and the answer still eludes me.

00:07:11.723 --> 00:07:14.320
It just makes no sense,
why would they do that?

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.850
- Of course, we also
had the Privilege War.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:19.780
For those who don't remember,
at the start of freshman year,

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.530
all the FroCo's had to
step forward if we ever had

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:26.010
some sort of privilege, and
I don't know about you Oscar,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.290
but for me, what a workout!

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:30.760
God, they had me moving a lot.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.390
Have you traveled to
more than five countries?

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:33.430
Yes I have!

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:34.890
Have you ever played tennis?

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.150
Let me step forwards!

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.660
Do you pronounce it "finance"?

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.200
I just did.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.923
Wow, I've never moved more.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.110
- Yeah I know, right?

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.020
Totally agree.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:45.353
- Wait, you moved too?

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.330
- Yeah, but they have me move backwards.

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:50.800
- There was Psych and the Good Life,

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.450
Yale's biggest ever
class, and the best part

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:56.480
about that class was that
no one did what they said

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.320
they would do in their
final paper proposal.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.440
I said I was going to
meditate for four hours a day

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.820
for two weeks, and then I sat
down for like five minutes

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.730
and got bored and opened Instagram.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:09.880
- What was that, Simon?

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.740
- Sorry, you're muted I think?

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.160
- I'm definitely not muted.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:14.993
- No, you are.

00:08:14.993 --> 00:08:17.440
- But then how did you know what I said?

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.373
- Sorry!
- Wait.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.020
- You know, I didn't take
Psych and the Good Life.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:24.973
- Oh, why's that?

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.790
- Intro ER&amp;M was at the
same, so no judgment

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:32.370
but I guess we have different
definitions of self-care.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:34.410
- We had the admissions
scandal which showed

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:37.183
how getting into college
isn't always a meritocracy.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.180
- But Vice didn't interview me about it

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:42.590
so in a way I guess I'm
richer than I thought,

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:45.230
kind of upper-middle working class?

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.720
Also I want to take this
time to say thank you

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.310
on behalf of myself and
all the other people

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.370
who also never sent a
letter to their donor,

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.683
so to my donor, thank you.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:58.490
- You're welcome.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:01.510
- Of course, coronavirus came
which has brought so much

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:05.060
unimaginable drama to an
already dramatic four years.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:06.660
Coronavirus taught us a lot.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.933
- For me it was learning just
how many people go on cruises.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:13.250
- And for me it was just how seriously

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:15.550
the Spring Fling committee takes its job.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.100
When the world had already
practically stopped,

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:20.100
the campus was shut down for good

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:23.650
and the CDC had banned gatherings
of more than 10 people,

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:26.240
the Spring Fling committee
sent out an email saying

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.270
they had tried every option but had made

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:33.100
the incredibly tough and
not obvious at all decision

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.640
to cancel Spring Fling,
and I applaud that spirit.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.800
- Coronavirus meant we also
lost out on a lot of things,

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:41.173
including Senior Week.

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:44.670
- Tonight would've been Erotica,

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.940
which for those who
don't know is one night

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.440
when the entire senior
class goes to Toad's

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.233
wearing only underwear.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:53.883
- Oscar?

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.100
- Yeah, sorry, I think I got muted.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.687
I was trying to say that Erotica is when--

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:02.523
- What is happening?

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.000
- Yeah, I don't think Yale
will let me describe Erotica.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:08.833
- Why not?

00:10:08.833 --> 00:10:11.637
All that is is when 1,000
seniors put on lingerie--

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:16.069
- They, yeah they don't, and there's--

00:10:16.069 --> 00:10:18.819
(audio cuts out)

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:30.060
So big senior pizza party.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:30.893
- There we go.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:33.730
Oscar, how are you
dealing with the effects

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.203
of coronavirus in L.A.?

00:10:36.195 --> 00:10:38.240
- Well, I was trying
to make the most of it

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.040
so I went to Universal Studios today

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:41.300
but they wouldn't let me in.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:42.850
- That makes sense, they
were probably closed

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.750
because of the virus.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.890
- Yeah, but I was like, I
don't think you understand.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:50.410
I have a Universal pass.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.420
I lobbied the Yale
administration for this.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:55.630
I emailed like the U.S.
but they still escorted me

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:58.500
from the premises and
threatened to arrest me.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.010
- No Oscar, "Universal Pass"
was the policy adopted by Yale

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.820
where all grades became pass/fail.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:08.045
But that didn't mean our
academic standards slipped.

00:11:08.045 --> 00:11:10.710
- Simon, I read an essay
you wrote, it was terrible.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.690
- Well, that's not true.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.307
- Your opening line was, "Emily Dickinson

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:17.490
"and Pablo Neruda were
two different people."

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:19.738
- Some people have doubts about that.

00:11:19.738 --> 00:11:21.730
- It was your final paper for Spanish.

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:22.563
- Your point?

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.500
- You wrote it in English.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.960
- Coronavirus also meant
that since people faced

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.490
different working
conditions away from campus,

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:34.340
some departments didn't award
prizes for senior theses,

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.370
but what the university
hasn't announced yet

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.200
is that because we were all
apart for these last few months,

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:42.610
those big prizes, the ones
for the best people at Yale,

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:44.140
they're gone too.

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:45.944
Sorry, Sedurin.

00:11:45.944 --> 00:11:49.100
- I guess we'll never know if
I was going to win the award

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:51.780
for Best Community Member
in Branford College.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.680
- Oscar, you're in Hopper.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:54.840
- I guess we'll never know.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.140
- No, we're kidding, there
will be the big prizes

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.520
but they don't always
cover every category.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.963
Therefore we have decided
to do our own prize giving.

00:12:03.945 --> 00:12:06.270
- First up for Most Piggybacks in Section

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.943
is everyone who took the S.

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.350
- Then it's not sunny too often,

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:13.940
but for the best tan congratulations

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.313
to Saybrook's own Tan Tan Wang.

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:22.700
- And finally a familiar
face for the Best Memer,

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.460
George Iskander.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.192
- It's a real honor to win this--

00:12:26.192 --> 00:12:27.275
- Bye George.

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:30.290
We left Yale too early

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:32.690
and we had different Yale experiences.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.410
Some of us never had the
Tuna Tartar at Harvest.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:37.570
- And some of us still
think Shades of Yale

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.733
is a Facebook group for
overheard insults on campus.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:44.420
- And it is all too easy to be engulfed

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.750
in the current moment,
but for as many bad things

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.320
as we've gone through, there have been

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.360
so many more good things.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:52.169
- We've shared late lunches at Commons

00:12:52.169 --> 00:12:54.767
and we've shared late nights at butteries.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:57.250
- We've shared photos on Facebook

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.660
and we've shared study
guides on Google Docs.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.220
- We've shared seats
at sold out dance shows

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:03.940
and we've shared utter contempt

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.473
for the other college in IMs.

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.480
- We've shared Franzia at parties

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.090
and some of us shared Froyo in the sacks

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:13.100
and although Yale was different for us all

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:16.030
and we all leave here having
our own personal connection

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:19.590
to it, we all leave sharing one thing:

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.060
the ugliest diploma in America.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.990
When you get it, just look at it

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:26.870
and remind yourself of this speech.

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:30.170
- So once this pandemic
ends and the next time

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:34.610
you're in New York or L.A. or New York

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:37.680
and you see another person
in the Class of 2020,

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.340
maybe you'll wave to them like you did

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.740
your entire time at
Yale after you met once

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:42.943
in the Zeta basement.

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:46.200
- But even if you don't, you'll still know

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.270
that both of you share being
part of the most iconic class

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:51.710
and community at Yale since 1010

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:53.890
and still have so much more to share.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.853
- Here's to the class of 2020.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:03.270
- Oh my God!

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:04.103
- We got it!

00:14:04.103 --> 00:14:06.489
That was crazy.

00:14:06.489 --> 00:14:09.130
I can't believe this
should be in the video too.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:11.553
I think we had it--
- That was so bad.

00:14:11.553 --> 00:14:14.240
- Thank God, Jesus Christ.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:15.890
- Oh my God.
- Two days of work.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:19.158
Whoa, that was psycho.

00:14:19.158 --> 00:14:20.750
- We got it, we got it.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:24.400
- No wonder 1917 won 'cause Jesus Christ,

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:26.853
to do anything in one shot, wow.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.560
Jeez.
- Oh my God, wow.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.240
That was--
- That was crazy.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.440
- This is joy.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.820
This is--
- This is, I've never felt

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.773
a rush of euphoria like this in a while.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.200
And now to start writing
the one we're gonna have

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:46.053
to deliver live next year.

